        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: prevent operational notices from leaking to source chats",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where users interacting with agents through external chats such as WhatsApp could see automatic runtime notices when model fallback, usage limits, compaction, backend failures, heartbeat checks, ACP status, plugin-binding failures, or tool/progress warnings happened.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThis adds `messages.operationalReplies` so operators can control automatic operational notices separately from normal assistant replies. The policy supports `always`, `silent`, `once`, and `redirect`; `redirect` writes notices to an operator session through `redirectSessionKey`, and `sendPolicy: deny` still wins.\n\nThe policy is applied across direct dispatch, ACP delivery, ACP status projection, queued follow-ups, compaction notices, route-failure notices, heartbeat delivery, progress/status callbacks, plugin-binding notices, tool warnings, and block/final replies. Normal message-tool replies, authorized command replies, before-run block replies, and background ACP child suppression keep their existing behavior.\n\nThe branch was rebased during follow-up, and the unrelated WhatsApp shrinkwrap delta was removed from the PR. The `once` policy now records delivered notice fingerprints on the source session, so duplicate suppression survives process restarts instead of relying only on an in-memory set.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators can keep fallback, usage-limit, backend-error, compaction, heartbeat, status, ACP, follow-up, plugin-binding, and tool-warning notices out of external chats while still preserving logs or redirecting those notices to an operator-owned session. Existing configs keep current behavior because the default policy is `always`.\n\n## Evidence\n\n### Real Behavior Proof: WhatsApp-like Source Chat\n\nEarlier redacted terminal proof on this branch used the actual `applyOperationalReplyPolicy` helper, a real temp `sessions.json`, and a source context shaped like an external WhatsApp direct chat:\n\n- source channel: `whatsapp`\n- source session: `agent:main:whatsapp:direct:redacted-user`\n- payload: `isError: true`, `isFallbackNotice: true`, text `model fallback failed; usage limit reached`\n\nObserved output:\n\n```text\nsilent: shouldDeliverToSourceChat=false, intentionalSilence=true, externalSends=0, opsTranscriptBytes=0\nredirect: shouldDeliverToSourceChat=false, redirected=true, externalSends=0, opsTranscriptHasSourceSession=true, opsTranscriptHasSourceChannel=true, opsTranscriptHasNoticeText=true\nredirect transcript excerpt: sourceSessionKey: agent:main:whatsapp:direct:redacted-user | sourceChannel: whatsapp | kind: error\n```\n\nThis proves the affected external-source path does not deliver the operational fallback/error notice back to the source chat under `silent`, and under `redirect` it keeps source chat sends at zero while writing the notice, source session, source channel, and notice kind into the configured operator session transcript.\n\n### Local Validation\n\nLatest follow-up validation after rebasing on current `origin/main` and pushing `ba8836de`:\n\n- `node scripts/check-session-accessor-boundary.mjs`\n- `node --import tsx scripts/generate-config-doc-baseline.ts --check`\n- `node --max-old-space-size=8192 --import tsx scripts/generate-plugin-sdk-api-baseline.ts --check`\n- `node --max-old-space-size=8192 --import tsx scripts/generate-plugin-sdk-api-baseline.ts --write` (to refresh the hash after `main` moved)\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.auto-reply-reply.config.ts src/auto-reply/reply/acp-projector.test.ts src/auto-reply/reply/followup-runner.test.ts src/auto-reply/reply/dispatch-from-config.test.ts src/auto-reply/reply/agent-runner.misc.runreplyagent.test.ts` (4 files, 538 tests)\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.tooling.config.ts test/scripts/plugin-sdk-surface-report.test.ts` (1 file, 9 tests)\n- `git diff --check`\n- `codex review --base origin/main`\n\nThe focused dispatch coverage now includes the durable `once` policy path and verifies suppressed plugin-binding route replies do not count as delivered before a later visible notice. The ACP projector truncation test asserts status-notice tagging, and the plugin SDK public surface budget is pinned to the current branch count.\n\nNot run after this rebase: full `pnpm test`, full `pnpm check`, or full `pnpm build`. A broad local `tsgo` test typecheck stalled without new output earlier, so CI should be treated as the source of truth for the full typecheck.\n\nAI-assisted: yes (Codex).\n\n\n\n",
        "branch": "operational-replies-policy",
